earned Debian its reputation for being ancient to
the point of point of fossilization, because the
subsequent release was tardy.  Woody came out
in 2002 and was still the "latest stable" Debian 3
years later in mid 2005, by which time Ubuntu had
acquired a great many of Debian's former users.
One year is a reasonable amount of time between
stable releases of a Linux distribution.  Much less
than a year makes you a "bleeding edge" distro.
A year and a half is okay, especially for a fairly
conservative distro like Debian stable.  Three
years, however, is far too long.)

